# Cursor Rules — Day-to-Day Engineering
> Prefer clarity over cleverness. When in doubt, choose the simplest thing that works.

## Scope / Tech Stack
- Backend: .NET (Minimal API), Node.js (Express.js)
- Frontend: Angular, React, TypeScript
- Databases: SQL Server, MySQL
- Cloud/DevOps: Azure, CI/CD (GitHub Actions), IaC (Bicep/Terraform)
- Observability & Analytics: Serilog, App Insights, PostHog

## What to Optimize For
- Maintainability for the “next maintainer” in 6+ months
- Correctness, security, and least surprise
- Small, readable units of code with explicit behavior
- Consistency over personal preference

---

## 1) General Engineering
**Do**
- Single-purpose functions/classes; small, readable units.
- Code a teammate can understand in 3 minutes.
- Prefer composition over inheritance; simple data structures.
- Be explicit (naming/structure); keep conventions consistent.
- Add short rationale in PRs for non-obvious choices.

**Do Not**
- Over-engineer abstractions before they’re needed.
- Hide side effects, use globals, or rely on “magic.”
- Add new tech that lacks a clear pain solved and an owner.

---

## 2) Security (Always On)
**Do**
- Validate & sanitize inputs; enforce server-side checks.
- Authenticate & authorize every request; assume hostile callers.
- Enforce **resource-level** authorization on every endpoint.
- Prevent IDOR/enumeration: never trust client IDs; scope on server; use opaque IDs on public endpoints.
- Use parameterized queries (Dapper/SQL Server; mysql2/Prisma for MySQL). Validate/escape identifiers.
- Keep secrets in env/Key Vault/secret store.

**Do Not**
- Rely on client checks or presence of an ID.
- Expose predictable sequential IDs without access checks.
- Log secrets/tokens/PII.
- Disable cert validation or relax CORS recklessly.
- Hardcode secrets/connection strings.

---

## 3) API Design (HTTP/Minimal API & Express)
**Do**
- Version endpoints (`/v1`), use proper HTTP codes; keep PUT/DELETE idempotent.
- Validate DTOs in service layer; return clear error models.
- Use correlation IDs; propagate across services.
- Support pagination/filter/sort with capped page sizes.
- Document with OpenAPI/Swagger; maintain examples.

**Do Not**
- Break backward compatibility without a deprecation plan.
- Leak internal exception details/stack traces.
- Build long-running synchronous endpoints.

**Express.js specifics**
- Use `helmet`, strict CORS, and rate limiting for public APIs.
- Centralize error-handling middleware; never expose raw stacks.
- Validate `req` (zod/joi/celebrate); reject unknown fields.
- Offload CPU-heavy work to queues/jobs.
- Set `trust proxy` behind load balancers; read client IP safely.
- Use DB connection pooling, reused across requests.

---

## 4) Data Access (SQL Server & MySQL)
**Do**
- Write explicit SQL with **named** parameters; review for N+1.
- Use transactions with explicit isolation where needed.
- Add indexes for frequent queries; inspect execution plans.
- Use connection pooling; reuse clients.
- MySQL: prefer InnoDB, `utf8mb4`, sane collations; avoid MyISAM.
- Time: set server/app to UTC; be explicit about timezones.
- Manage schema via migrations/scripts (Flyway/Prisma); review for locks.
- Prefer splitting overly complex SQL into smaller queries and combine in app code if it improves readability/maintainability.

**Do Not**
- `SELECT *` or depend on column order.
- Build dynamic SQL from user input.
- Rely on implicit timezone/collation.
- Ship unreadable multi-join monsters—split if needed.
- Duplicate query/logic across outputs (PDF vs Website); share SQL & C# logic (DRY).

---

## 5) Frontend (Angular + React + TypeScript)
**General**
- Enable **strict TypeScript**.
- Keep components small; accessible (labels, roles, focus).

**Angular**
- Use standalone components/feature modules; lazy-load routes.
- `OnPush` change detection, `HttpInterceptors`, route guards.
- Keep components thin; move business logic to services.
- Prefer RxJS with clear lifecycles (`takeUntil`, `firstValueFrom` only when appropriate). Avoid deep nested Observables.

**React**
- Functional components + hooks; memoize when measured; Error Boundaries.
- Never mutate props/state; avoid long sync blocks on UI thread.

**UI/UX for Business Apps**
**Do**
- Two-level validation: field (on blur) + form (on submit). Keep submit enabled; show error summary at top.
- Inline error text; mark invalid with `aria-invalid`; associate via `aria-describedby`; move focus to first error on submit.
- OTP inputs: segmented, auto-advance/backspace, allow paste/auto-fill, WebOTP if available; resend with short cooldown.
- Date pickers localized; display DD/MM/YYYY; parse pasted dates; enforce min/max; preserve timezone context. Ranges: presets (Last 7/30/90) with explicit inclusive/exclusive labels.
- Prefer Tailwind or Material; stick to design tokens; 4/8-pt spacing; visible focus; WCAG AA.
- Responsive tables: sticky headers, column resize, multi-select, bulk actions; persist filters/sort in URL.
- Feedback: skeletons < 1s; spinner/progress for longer; toasts success/info; inline errors for failures.
- RTL (Hebrew): logical props (me/ms), mirrored icons, locale-aware number/date.
- Keyboard support; 44×44px minimum targets.
- Server-side paging for lists/tables **over 100 records**.

**Do Not**
- Validate while typing (except constrained masks like phone/OTP).
- Accept free-text dates without parse/validate.
- Block paste or auto-correct OTP digits; don’t hide resend.
- Ship destructive actions without confirm/undo.
- Typically page **entity items (order items/invoice items/shipment items)**—avoid paging there.

**Authentication UX (OTP-first)**
- Default 6-digit OTP; paste/autofill; show remaining time; throttle attempts; clear “Resend code.”
- Provide fallback (magic link or TOTP). Remember trusted devices with explicit consent.
- Never reveal whether a user exists (“If an account exists, we’ll send a code”).

---

## 6) Testing
**Do**
- Unit tests for business logic; integration tests for DB/API; E2E for key flows (multi-feature).
- Mock only at boundaries; seed realistic data.
- Keep tests deterministic & fast; run in CI.
- Add regression tests for every bug fix.

**Do Not**
- Test brittle implementation details.
- Skip flaky tests—quarantine and fix promptly.
- Depend on network/external services in unit tests.

---

## 7) Bug Resolving
- Reproduce with user steps first; always reproduce locally.
- Add/extend E2E tests to cover the workflow.
- Prefer smart logging & monitoring over remote access to user machines.
- Follow exact action order; document in project Wiki.
- Challenge assumptions respectfully; request time to prove with data.
- Guard against biases: ask for alternative causes; revisit anchors.

---

## 8) CI/CD & Branching
**Do**
- Build CI/CD **first** (build, test, security scans, deploy to staging) before features.
- PRs for all changes; require review + green checks.
- Branch model: `develop → staging`, `main → production`; tag releases.
- Store pipelines in repo; protect branches.
- Promote artifacts; keep env parity.

**Workflow**
1. Branch from `main`:
2. Naming:
   - Features: `feature/#tasknumber-description`
     - e.g., `feature/#123-add-user-authentication`
   - Bug fixes: `bugfix/#tasknumber-description`
     - e.g., `bugfix/#234-fix-login-validation-error`
   - Use lowercase, hyphens, include task number.
3. Develop changes.
4. Add tests.
5. Selectively stage files; review diffs.
6. Commit to feature branch.
7. Push branch.
8. Switch to `develop`.
9. Refresh local `develop`.
10. Merge feature branch.
11. Push `develop`.
12. Open PR (all checks green).
13. If rejected, iterate (steps 2–12).
14. If no CD, deploy `main` to prod manually after PR approval.

**Do Not**
- Start feature work before CI/CD exists.
- Commit directly to `main`/`develop`.
- Change prod config via portal with no tracked change.
- Store secrets in pipeline YAML (use secret stores).

---

## 9) Observability & Analytics (Serilog + App Insights + PostHog)
**Do**
- Structured logs with correlation/user/request IDs.
- Emit metrics (latency/throughput/error rates) & dashboards.
- Set SLOs & alerts for key endpoints/jobs.
- Redact PII; sample high-volume logs.
- Instrument PostHog with stable taxonomy; tag env & app version.
- Separate PostHog projects per env; mask inputs; redact sensitive selectors.
- Respect consent/DNT; proxy via first-party backend when filtering is required.

**Do Not**
- Use `Console.WriteLine` in production.
- Log at Error for expected flows.
- Spam logs/metrics without sampling/aggregation.
- Send PII/secrets to PostHog; avoid free-text properties.
- Mix dev/staging traffic with production analytics.

---

## 10) Reliability & Resilience
**Do**
- Timeouts; retries with backoff (for transient faults).
- Circuit breakers, bulkheads; cap concurrency.
- Idempotent operations (webhooks/functions especially).
- Use queues (Service Bus/Storage) for decoupling/back-pressure.

**Do Not**
- Swallow exceptions or rethrow without context.
- Retry indefinitely—cap attempts and alert.
- Depend on single-region resources for HA needs.

---

## 11) Performance
**Do**
- Measure before optimizing; use profilers & App Insights traces.
- Batch work; paginate; cache with explicit TTL + invalidation.
- Use async I/O; avoid sync blocking on async.

**Do Not**
- Create N+1 queries/chatty APIs.
- Cache sensitive/highly dynamic data without strategy.

---

## 12) Cloud & Azure
**Do**
- Least privilege (RBAC); segment with resource groups.
- Key Vault, Managed Identity, Private Endpoints where applicable.
- Enforce policies (SKU allowlists, location, tagging, diagnostics).
- Use IaC (Bicep/Terraform); parameterize regions/SKUs.

**Do Not**
- Expose public endpoints by default.
- Store files locally—use Blob/File storage.
- Hardcode regions/SKUs (use IaC params).

---

## 13) Feature Flags & Experiments
**Do**
- Use PostHog feature flags for risky changes; define owner, rollout, kill switch.
- Record flag state in logs/telemetry; clean up after rollout.
- For A/B: define hypothesis, primary metric, min sample size, stop rule in PostHog.

**Do Not**
- Leave stale flags in code.
- Gate security/authorization solely behind flags.
- Run experiments without success metrics/guardrails.

---

## 14) Documentation & Knowledge
**Do**
- Maintain README, quickstart, ADRs for important decisions.
- Update API docs/examples with each change.
- Write runbooks for jobs/functions and on-call playbooks.

**Do Not**
- Merge behavior-changing code without updating docs.

---

## 15) Code Style & Tooling
**Do**
- C#: enable nullable, analyzers, warnings as errors; standard .NET naming.
- JS/TS: ESLint + Prettier; strict TS; avoid `any`.
- Keep consistent folder structures; use conventional commits.

**Do Not**
- Disable linters without justification.
- Mix unrelated changes in one PR; keep diffs small.

---

## 16) Privacy & Compliance
**Do**
- Classify data; minimize collection; pseudonymize where possible.
- Honor deletion/retention; scrub request/response logs of PII.

**Do Not**
- Send PII to third-party analytics without approval.
- Use production data in non-prod.

---

## 17) Pull Request Checklist (Quick)
**Reviewer mindset:** Assume you’re the next maintainer.

**Do**
- Self-review; link issue; explain approach.
- Add/update tests; run lints/build locally.
- Update docs/configs/migrations.
- Check for query/logic duplication across outputs (PDF vs Website); prefer shared code.
- Prefer clarity: split complex SQL into smaller queries + combine in app code if easier to reason about.

**Do Not**
- Open giant PRs—split into reviewable slices.
- Merge with failing checks or unresolved comments.
- Approve unreadable SQL or duplicated business logic.

---

## 18) Exceptions to Rules
**Do**
- Document exception, scope, owner, and sunset date in the PR.

**Do Not**
- Create silent one-offs that become the new default.

---

## Assistant Behavior in Cursor
- Generate code in English.
- Default to secure, production-ready patterns (headers, CORS, validation).
- Propose simplest viable solution first; call out trade-offs.
- When unsure, choose explicitness and maintainability over clever tricks.
